      <p class="page-kicker">CITY HALL · COUNCIL AGENDA · NOVEMBER 17, 2026</p>
      <h1>Read Before Tonight's City Council Meeting: Fire Overtime, a $5.3M Traffic Project, and Contract Questions</h1>
      <p class="article-dek">A closer look at West Covina's first-quarter finances, overtime spending, traffic improvements, and engineering contract oversight before the November 17 meeting.</p>
      <p class="story-byline">By StayJustIn · November 17, 2026</p>
    </header>
    <div class="story-content">
<p>🚨 WEST COVINA: Before tonight's City Council meeting, there are a few things in the City's reports I think residents should know about.</p>
<p>Nearly $22 million more spent than collected in the first quarter. A Fire Department that's already used about 95% of its annual overtime budget. And a $5.3 million traffic project involving a company that's already working on several other City projects.</p>
<p>Now, before anyone starts looking for the pitchforks, there's more to these numbers than meets the eye.</p>
<p>I've been going through the November 17 City Council agenda, reviewing contracts, looking into the companies involved, and comparing what I've found with previous City records.</p>
<p>Here's what caught my attention.</p>
<h2>💰 $21.9 MILLION — WHERE DID THE MONEY GO?</h2>
<p>The City's first-quarter financial report shows approximately $5.9 million in revenue and $27.8 million in expenditures.</p>
<p>Before anyone starts putting City Hall on Craigslist, this does NOT mean West Covina is $21.9 million in debt.</p>
<p>Much of the City's tax revenue comes in later throughout the year, so comparing just the first three months doesn't give us the full picture.</p>
<p>But here's what I find more concerning.</p>
<p>The City's annual General Fund budget is nearly $100 million, with a projected surplus of only about $108,000.</p>
<figure class="data-chart" aria-label="First quarter General Fund: revenue 5.9 million dollars and expenditures 27.8 million dollars"><figcaption>General Fund · First quarter</figcaption><div class="bar"><span>Revenue received</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:21.2%"></div></div><strong>$5.9M</strong></div><div class="bar"><span>Expenditures</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:100%"></div></div><strong>$27.8M</strong></div><small>Revenue arrives unevenly throughout the year. The $21.9M difference is not an annual deficit or debt figure.</small></figure>
<p>That's a pretty small cushion for a budget that size.</p>
<h2>🚒 THE FIRE DEPARTMENT'S OVERTIME SPENDING</h2>
<p>The financial report shows approximately $1.12 million in Fire Department overtime spending against an annual budget of $1.17 million.</p>
<p>That's roughly 95% of the entire year's overtime budget in just the first three months.</p>
<figure class="data-chart" aria-label="Fire overtime spent 1.12 million dollars out of 1.17 million dollars budgeted, approximately 95 percent"><figcaption>Fire overtime · Annual budget usage after first quarter</figcaption><div class="bar"><span>Spent</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:95%"></div></div><strong>~95%</strong></div><small>$1.12M spent of $1.17M budgeted. Calculated from reported dollar figures; the City's percentage table contains an apparent mismatch.</small></figure>
<p>Now, there could be reasonable explanations. Staffing shortages, emergency responses, or how certain expenses are recorded could all play a role.</p>
<p>But I think residents deserve to know what's driving those costs and how the City plans to cover overtime for the remaining nine months.</p>
<h2>🚦 A $5.3 MILLION TRAFFIC PROJECT — BUT THERE'S MORE TO THIS CONTRACT THAN THE PRICE.</h2>
<p>West Covina is considering traffic-safety improvements involving 64 intersections throughout the city.</p>
<p>The good news? Federal funding is expected to cover 90% of the $5.26 million project, leaving approximately $526,000 in local transportation funding.</p>
<figure class="data-chart" aria-label="Traffic project funding: 90 percent federal and 10 percent local"><figcaption>$5.26M traffic project · Funding sources</figcaption><div class="stack"><div class="fed" style="width:90%"></div><div class="loc" style="width:10%"></div></div><div class="legend"><span>Federal · $4,733,190 (90%)</span><span>Local · $525,910 (10%)</span></div><small>Federal reimbursement is subject to program requirements.</small></figure>
<p>Sounds like a good deal, right?</p>
<p>But I started looking a little deeper into the engineering contract.</p>
<p>Six companies submitted proposals.</p>
<p>The highest-ranked company, Kimley-Horn, scored 87.67 points.</p>
<p>The second-place company scored 87.33.</p>
<p>That's a difference of just 0.34 points.</p>
<figure class="data-chart" aria-label="Kimley-Horn scored 87.67 and the second-place engineering company scored 87.33"><figcaption>Engineering consultant evaluation · Score out of 100</figcaption><div class="bar"><span>Kimley-Horn</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:87.67%"></div></div><strong>87.67</strong></div><div class="bar"><span>Second place</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:87.33%"></div></div><strong>87.33</strong></div><small>Qualifications-based scoring, not a comparison of bid prices. Difference: 0.34 points.</small></figure>
<p><strong>Now, here's something important that I learned while looking into this.</strong></p>
<p>Because federal transportation funding is involved, engineering firms are generally selected based on their qualifications first, with the price negotiated afterward.</p>
<p>So this isn't necessarily a case of the City passing up a cheaper company.</p>
<p><strong>But it does raise another question.</strong></p>
<p>How did the City determine that the nearly $360,000 engineering contract was a fair price?</p>
<p><strong>And there's something else.</strong></p>
<p>Kimley-Horn isn't new to West Covina.</p>
<p>The company's proposal identifies four other City projects it's worked on or is currently involved with, including traffic signals, pedestrian safety improvements, and work along Azusa Avenue.</p>
<p>In July 2025, Council approved another Kimley-Horn engineering agreement for approximately $544,000.</p>
<p>That agreement also listed roughly $90,000 in optional work.</p>
<p>During that meeting, Council members raised concerns about using Measure M funds when approximately $7 million in federal funding had reportedly been secured for the community.</p>
<p>Staff said they were working with Caltrans to understand the funding requirements.</p>
<p>What I haven't been able to confirm yet is whether that funding was eventually used, whether the optional work was approved, or whether the original contract amount changed.</p>
<p><strong>And that brings me to something I think deserves more attention.</strong></p>
<p>Council is now being asked to let the City Manager negotiate and approve future amendments to this new engineering agreement.</p>
<p><strong>Why does that matter?</strong></p>
<p>Imagine hiring someone to remodel your kitchen for $36,000.</p>
<p>You agree on the price, but later additional work is needed.</p>
<p>Maybe it's necessary. Maybe the cost is reasonable.</p>
<p>But wouldn't you want to know who gets to approve the extra charges and how much they can authorize before coming back to you?</p>
<p>That's essentially what I'm asking here.</p>
<p>The proposed agreement has an original spending limit, but the Council recommendation doesn't spell out a separate dollar limit for future amendments.</p>
<p>Other City rules may limit that authority. I want to know what those limits actually are.</p>
<p>And with federal grants, there's another concern. The City generally has to meet the program's requirements to get reimbursed. Federal funding doesn't automatically cover every additional expense.</p>
<p>None of this proves anything improper happened.</p>
<p>But considering that the State Auditor previously identified weaknesses in West Covina's contract oversight, I think these are fair questions to ask before approving another agreement.</p>
<section class="police-equipment-story" aria-label="Police military equipment report comparison">
<h2>West Covina Police Drone Use Jumps From 8 to 97 Deployments in One Year. Here's What the City's Reports Reveal.</h2>
<p>Police military-equipment spending also increased from approximately $10,800 to nearly $79,000. But there's more to these numbers than meets the eye.</p>
<p>West Covina's Police Department is asking City Council to review its annual military-equipment report, and after going through the documents from the past two years, a few things caught my attention.</p>
<p>For starters, police drone deployments increased from just 8 in 2024 to 97 in 2025.</p>
<p>That's more than twelve times as many deployments in one year.</p>
<p>And spending on equipment covered under California's military-equipment reporting law increased by more than 600%.</p>
<p>Now, before anyone starts imagining tanks rolling down Azusa Avenue, there's something important to understand.</p>
<p>California has a pretty broad definition of what it considers military equipment.</p>
<p>It doesn't necessarily mean the equipment came from the military.</p>
<p>The law includes things like police drones, armored rescue vehicles, specialized firearms, flashbang devices, and certain less-lethal weapons.</p>
<p>Some of these tools can serve an important purpose, especially when officers are dealing with dangerous situations.</p>
<p>But I think residents should also understand what equipment our Police Department owns, how often it's being used, and what it's costing.</p>
<p>So I decided to compare the City's 2024 and 2025 reports.</p>
<p>And here's what I found.</p>
<h3>🚁 POLICE DRONE DEPLOYMENTS INCREASED MORE THAN 1,100%</h3>
<p>According to the Police Department's annual reports:</p>
<div class="source-lines"><p>- 2024: 8 drone deployments and 10 training sessions.</p>
<p>- 2025: 97 drone deployments and 10 training sessions.</p></div>
<p>That's an increase of 89 deployments, or approximately 1,113%.</p>
<figure class="data-chart"><figcaption>Police drone deployments by year</figcaption><div class="bar"><span>2024</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:8.25%"></div></div><strong>8</strong></div><div class="bar"><span>2025</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:100%"></div></div><strong>97</strong></div><small>Increase: 89 deployments (approximately 1,113%). Training sessions: 10 each year.</small></figure>
<p>Think about that for a moment.</p>
<p>In 2024, the department reported using its drones in the field fewer than once a month, on average.</p>
<p>In 2025, that increased to roughly eight times a month.</p>
<p>Now, does that mean something improper is happening?</p>
<p>Not necessarily.</p>
<p>Drones can be useful for searching for missing people, locating suspects, responding to emergencies, or helping officers assess dangerous situations without putting themselves directly in harm's way.</p>
<p>There are legitimate reasons for police departments to use this technology.</p>
<p>But here's what I couldn't find in these reports.</p>
<p>What were those 97 deployments actually for?</p>
<p>How many involved emergencies? How many involved searches? Were any used for routine patrol or other police operations?</p>
<p>The reports provide the total number of deployments, but they don't break down the reasons behind them.</p>
<p>And when the use of a technology increases that much in a single year, I think it's reasonable to ask what's driving the change.</p>
<h3>💰 MILITARY-EQUIPMENT SPENDING INCREASED BY MORE THAN 600%</h3>
<p>The Police Department reported spending the following amounts on equipment covered by California's AB 481 law:</p>
<div class="source-lines"><p>Year| Reported Spending</p>
<p>2024| $10,773.31</p>
<p>2025| $78,895.44</p>
<p>Two-year total| $89,668.75</p></div>
<p>That's an increase of approximately 633%.</p>
<figure class="data-chart"><figcaption>AB 481 reported equipment spending</figcaption><div class="bar"><span>2024</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:13.66%"></div></div><strong>$10.8K</strong></div><div class="bar"><span>2025</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:100%"></div></div><strong>$78.9K</strong></div><small>Reported spending increased by approximately 633%.</small></figure>
<p>At first glance, that sounds like a pretty dramatic jump.</p>
<p>But here's where looking at the details matters.</p>
<p>Most of the increase came from two purchases.</p>
<p>The department reported spending $59,940.24 to replace its outdated drone fleet and another $13,913.75 on a kinetic breaching tool.</p>
<figure class="data-chart"><figcaption>Major reported 2025 purchases</figcaption><div class="bar"><span>Drones</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:100%"></div></div><strong>$59,940</strong></div><div class="bar"><span>Breaching tool</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:23.21%"></div></div><strong>$13,914</strong></div><small>Amounts are from the equipment report and do not represent the department's entire equipment budget.</small></figure>
<p>For anyone unfamiliar, a breaching tool is equipment officers can use to force open locked doors during certain operations.</p>
<p>The remaining reported expenses included maintenance for the BearCat armored vehicle, maintenance for the mobile command post, and replacement flashbang equipment.</p>
<p>There's another detail worth pointing out.</p>
<p>The drone spending was listed under federal asset-sharing funds, while the other expenses were listed under the City's General Fund.</p>
<p>So it wouldn't be accurate to say the entire $78,895 came directly out of the City's regular operating budget.</p>
<p>Still, regardless of where the money comes from, I think the public deserves to know how it's being spent.</p>
<p>And these figures only cover the equipment included in the military-equipment reports. They aren't the Police Department's entire equipment budget.</p>
<h3>🚁 HOW MANY DRONES DOES WEST COVINA OWN?</h3>
<p>Here's something else I found interesting.</p>
<p>The department's 2025 equipment inventory lists 24 drones across several different models.</p>
<p>Some are smaller drones, while others have equipment such as thermal cameras, zoom lenses, spotlights, and speakers.</p>
<p>These features can help officers search areas, locate people, and respond to situations from a safer distance.</p>
<p>But having 24 drones listed in an inventory doesn't necessarily mean all 24 are currently operational.</p>
<p>And the report doesn't explain which drones were replaced, which remain in service, or whether any of the older ones were taken out of use.</p>
<p>Considering the City spent nearly $60,000 replacing outdated drones, those are details worth knowing.</p>
<h3>🚓 WEST COVINA ALSO OWNS THREE ARMORED VEHICLES</h3>
<p>The department's inventory includes:</p>
<div class="source-lines"><p>- A Mine Resistant Ambush Protected vehicle, commonly called an MRAP.</p>
<p>- A Cadillac Gage Peacekeeper.</p>
<p>- A Lenco BearCat armored rescue vehicle.</p></div>
<p>These vehicles are designed to provide protection during dangerous situations, including incidents involving armed suspects or emergency rescues.</p>
<p>The reports show the BearCat was deployed seven times in 2024 and five times in 2025.</p>
<figure class="data-chart"><figcaption>BearCat reported field deployments</figcaption><div class="bar"><span>2024</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:100%"></div></div><strong>7</strong></div><div class="bar"><span>2025</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:71.43%"></div></div><strong>5</strong></div><small>MRAP and Peacekeeper: zero reported field deployments in both years.</small></figure>
<p>The MRAP and Peacekeeper had no reported field deployments during either year.</p>
<p>Now, I understand that emergency equipment isn't necessarily something you want to use every day.</p>
<p>A fire extinguisher sitting unused on the wall doesn't mean it was a waste of money.</p>
<p>The same reasoning can apply to certain police equipment.</p>
<p>But there's still a reasonable question about how much it costs to maintain equipment that may rarely be used.</p>
<p>And there's something else.</p>
<p>In its 2024 report, the Police Department said it wanted to acquire another armored vehicle and another drone if funding became available.</p>
<p>The 2025 report doesn't identify an additional armored vehicle in its inventory, and it says there are no plans to acquire more equipment the following year.</p>
<p>So what happened to that earlier proposal?</p>
<p>Was it postponed? Was it no longer needed? Or did the department decide to use the equipment it already had?</p>
<p>I haven't been able to confirm that yet.</p>
<h3>👮 WHAT ABOUT COMPLAINTS OR POLICY VIOLATIONS?</h3>
<p>Here's something that should also be included when looking at the full picture.</p>
<p>The Police Department reported receiving no complaints or concerns about its military equipment in either 2024 or 2025.</p>
<p>It also reported conducting internal audits and finding no violations of its military-equipment policy.</p>
<p>That's important information.</p>
<p>But there's a difference between a department reporting no complaints and independently confirming that every deployment followed policy.</p>
<p>I'm not suggesting violations occurred.</p>
<p>I'm simply pointing out that the annual reports give us the department's findings, without providing the individual deployment records or detailed audit results needed to review those findings ourselves.</p>
<h3>📄 I ALSO FOUND SOMETHING THAT NEEDS CLARIFICATION IN THE 2025 REPORT</h3>
<p>While reviewing the documents, I noticed the 2025 report contains what appears to be an error.</p>
<p>The report clearly states that it covers January 1 through December 31, 2025.</p>
<p>But the spending table is labeled 2024 Military Equipment Use, and its funding column also refers to 2024.</p>
<p>Could this simply be a copy-and-paste mistake from the previous year's report?</p>
<p>Absolutely.</p>
<p>But when these reports are being used to inform City Council and the public, the information should be accurate and easy to understand.</p>
<p>I'd like the department to confirm that the spending and deployment numbers in the table are indeed for 2025.</p>
<h3>🏛️ HERE'S WHY THIS MATTERS FOR CITY COUNCIL</h3>
<p>California's AB 481 law requires police departments to report how certain equipment is being used, what it costs, what they own, and what they plan to purchase.</p>
<p>It also gives the public an opportunity to ask questions.</p>
<p>The purpose isn't simply to approve or reject police equipment.</p>
<p>It's to make sure the decisions are being made openly, with enough information for residents and elected officials to understand them.</p>
<p>And I think that's especially important when the numbers show a major change from one year to the next.</p>
<p>The Police Department has scheduled a public meeting for Tuesday, November 24, 2026, at 5 PM in the West Covina Council Chambers.</p>
<p>Residents will have an opportunity to ask questions about the department's military equipment, including how it's funded and used.</p>
<h3>HERE'S WHAT I'D LIKE TO KNOW</h3>
<p>After reviewing both reports, these are the questions I think deserve answers:</p>
<div class="source-lines"><p>1. Why did drone deployments increase from 8 to 97 in one year? What types of incidents accounted for that increase?</p></div>
<p>2. How many of the 24 drones listed in the inventory are currently operational? Which ones were replaced with the nearly $60,000 spent in 2025?</p>
<p>3. What happened to the department's 2024 plan to acquire another armored vehicle? Was that purchase canceled, postponed, or simply no longer needed?</p>
<p>4. How does the department measure whether its equipment is helping improve public safety? Are there results residents can review beyond the number of deployments?</p>
<p>5. Can the department confirm that the 2025 report's spending and deployment figures are correct, despite the table still referring to 2024?</p>
<h3>MY TAKEAWAY</h3>
<p>I'm not against our Police Department having the tools it needs to do its job.</p>
<p>If a drone can help find a missing child, locate someone in danger, or keep an officer from walking blindly into a dangerous situation, I can understand the value.</p>
<p>And if an armored vehicle helps protect officers and residents during an emergency, that matters too.</p>
<p>But supporting public safety and asking questions about public spending aren't opposites.</p>
<p>We can do both.</p>
<p>The City's reports show a major increase in drone deployments and equipment spending. They also explain much of the spending increase.</p>
<p>What they don't fully explain is why drone use increased so dramatically, what those deployments involved, and how the department measures the results.</p>
<p>That's where I think the conversation should go next.</p>
<p>I'm interested in hearing what other residents think.</p>
<p>Does the increase in drone deployments concern you, or do you see it as a positive sign that the department is making better use of technology?</p>
<p>And what information would you want to see before making that judgment?</p>
<p>You don't have to agree with my questions. I'd just like us to have enough information to make up our own minds.</p>
<p>Because whether we're supporting City Hall's decisions or questioning them, we should all be working from the same facts.</p>
<p>Stay informed. Stay involved. StayJustIn.</p>
